       $alt$Editorial illustration of a drive-thru coffee stand replacing a closed restaurant in suburban Texas.$alt$::text image_alt_text,
       $body$A restaurant bankruptcy is becoming a real-estate opportunity for Dutch Bros Coffee, which is set to take over dozens of former Salad and Go locations, including sites in Katy and Sugar Land. The swap is a particularly visible example of how quickly suburban retail can change: one drive-thru concept disappears, another inherits a property already designed around cars, queues and quick service. For [Houston](/houston) customers, the change will mean more coffee options. For [Texas Business](/texas-business), it is a case study in how chains can expand faster by reusing existing restaurant pads instead of starting every location from raw land.

Salad and Go had already pulled out of Texas earlier in 2026 before filing for Chapter 11 bankruptcy and shutting remaining stores in other states. Its former sites have qualities other restaurant operators value: established curb cuts, drive-thru lanes, utility connections, signage positions and locations chosen for commuter traffic. Those features can take months or years to secure in a new development. If Dutch Bros can adapt the buildings without major reconstruction, it can shorten the path from lease or acquisition to opening day.

The strategy fits Dutch Bros unusually well because the company’s model emphasizes beverages and drive-thru service. A coffee stand does not need the same kitchen, dining room or storage layout as a salad restaurant. That means the company can potentially shrink, reconfigure or repurpose spaces while keeping the most valuable part of the site—the circulation pattern. In fast-growing Houston suburbs, a corner with strong traffic counts and easy vehicle access can be worth more than the building sitting on it.

Katy and Sugar Land are logical expansion markets. Both have large populations, heavy commuter movement and a retail landscape built around repeat convenience trips. Coffee chains benefit from habit: customers stop on the way to work, school or errands, often several times a week. Once a brand establishes a dense enough network, convenience becomes self-reinforcing because a customer can find the same menu across multiple routes. Dutch Bros has been building that density in Texas, and inherited drive-thru sites accelerate the process.

The story also shows the limits of “good real estate” as a guarantee of restaurant success. Salad and Go’s difficulties were not necessarily a verdict on every location it occupied. Brand strategy, menu demand, operating costs, financing and expansion pace can all determine whether a chain survives. When a tenant fails, the property can still be attractive to another operator with a different product and cost structure. That is why landlords and competitors watch bankruptcies closely: distressed chains can release prime locations that rarely become available in normal market conditions.

For customers, the transition may feel almost instantaneous compared with traditional development. New construction is easy to spot for months, while a conversion can move from a closed sign to a new brand quickly once permits and remodeling begin. That speed is part of the business case. Every month saved on site development can reduce carrying costs and allow the new operator to begin generating revenue sooner. The existing neighborhood also already knows the location as a place to enter, queue and exit by car.

There are still risks. A layout optimized for salads may not perfectly fit coffee throughput, and traffic patterns that worked for one volume level may struggle if the new brand draws longer morning queues. Cities will examine permits, stacking capacity and any proposed changes to access. Neighbors may be more sensitive if a conversion increases early-morning or late-evening traffic. Reusing a restaurant pad is faster than building from scratch, but it does not erase local development rules.

The broader trend is likely to continue as restaurant chains expand and contract more quickly. Texas population growth creates demand, but it also attracts intense competition. A site may cycle through multiple concepts over a decade while the underlying drive-thru infrastructure remains valuable. Operators with strong balance sheets can use downturns or bankruptcies to pick up locations that would otherwise be difficult to assemble. That can make a failed chain’s footprint an expansion map for the next brand.

Chron’s report on the Dutch Bros deal captures that cycle clearly. Salad and Go’s retreat left a network of purpose-built properties; Dutch Bros sees a shortcut to growth. In Katy and Sugar Land, residents will soon see the most concrete version of that strategy as familiar sites change identities. The buildings may look different, but the commercial logic is the same: capture a convenient corner, move vehicles efficiently and turn a daily habit into repeat business.

The conversion also reflects how beverage brands can support different economics than food-heavy concepts. Coffee and flavored drinks often carry strong margins, require a narrower ingredient set and can be produced quickly once a store is designed for throughput. That does not make the business easy—labor, rent and customer acquisition still matter—but it can make a compact drive-thru location highly productive. Dutch Bros has built its identity around speed, customization and an energetic service style. Former Salad and Go sites give the company a chance to apply that model in locations where customers are already trained to make quick drive-thru stops.$body$::text body,
       $links$[{"href":"/houston","kind":"hub","label":"Houston"},{"href":"/texas-business","kind":"hub","label":"Texas Business"}]$links$::jsonb internal_links
